    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mandaeans believe in one God, Hayyi Rabbi, the Great Life, whose symbol is living water, and for that reason they need to live near rivers; baptism in flowing water is performed every Sunday.</li><li>Mandaeans revere Adam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a>, Shem and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, whom they hold to be the greatest and final prophet, and they regard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as a false prophet.</li><li>Mandaeism does not allow conversion, and the community has shrunk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>: by 2007 about 5,000 Mandaeans remained there, as many fled the turmoil that followed the invasion of 2003.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Mandaeans call every river fit for baptism Yardena, after the River Jordan, and every mandi, their place of worship, must stand beside a river or, in modern buildings, hold a bath in its place.</p></aside>
    </section>

